(621140, 64481, 6888, '<td valign="top">I like the pose here...the colors are good and so is the focus...I may have gone for a tighter crop and gottten rid of some of the brick wall</td>', 'I like the pose here...the colors are good and so is the focus...I may have gone for a tighter crop and gottten rid of some of the brick wall', '2004-03-15 00:40:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(621141, 64451, 6804, '<td valign="top">This is my favorite portrait of the challenge so far. This is perfect work. The subject is more than just a face. This tells the story of the person.  Nice simple color with formal black drops that help keep your attention on the subject. The only thing this needs is your signature :)   Beautiful work!    10</td>', 'This is my favorite portrait of the challenge so far. This is perfect work. The subject is more than just a face. This tells the story of the person.  Nice simple color with formal black drops that help keep your attention on the subject. The only thing this needs is your signature :)   Beautiful work!    10', '2004-03-15 00:40:41.000000', NULL, 1),
(621142, 64647, 6888, '<td valign="top">has a nice soft feel to it and that works ok for a baby picture I think...there is a piece of something that looks as if it is coming out the top of his head the kind of pulls my eye away</td>', 'has a nice soft feel to it and that works ok for a baby picture I think...there is a piece of something that looks as if it is coming out the top of his head the kind of pulls my eye away', '2004-03-15 00:41:11.000000', NULL, 1),
(621145, 64562, 9232, '<td valign="top">Well, this starts off as a good portrait capture but it ends up with a dog in it . . . no serioiusly I hope you read past that after that big blowup that went on before the contest began.  The technical problems I see here are that the eyes are so dark we get almost no catchlights in them and there is a distracting object on the right of the photo.  Believe it or not both of these things are fixable so that makes them a real pain.  If you have PS you can use the Polygon tool to select around one eye and then hold the shift key and select around the other eye.  Now on the layers menu you choose the button that looks like a circle with a diagonal line through it and add a Curves layer based on this selection.  You can now lighten just the eyes to bring out more definition and show off some of those eyes that will make this a more personal shot to any viewer and not just someone that knows the pretty pooch.  Also with the object on photo right, you can simply clone it out and if you notice a gradiation flowing down your image use the Healing Brush rather than the Clone Stamp to gradually remove the offending outline.  I do like the overall dreamy quality you got.</td>', 'Well, this starts off as a good portrait capture but it ends up with a dog in it . . . no serioiusly I hope you read past that after that big blowup that went on before the contest began.  The technical problems I see here are that the eyes are so dark we get almost no catchlights in them and there is a distracting object on the right of the photo.  Believe it or not both of these things are fixable so that makes them a real pain.  If you have PS you can use the Polygon tool to select around one eye and then hold the shift key and select around the other eye.  Now on the layers menu you choose the button that looks like a circle with a diagonal line through it and add a Curves layer based on this selection.  You can now lighten just the eyes to bring out more definition and show off some of those eyes that will make this a more personal shot to any viewer and not just someone that knows the pretty pooch.  Also with the object on photo right, you can simply clone it out and if you notice a gradiation flowing down your image use the Healing Brush rather than the Clone Stamp to gradually remove the offending outline.  I do like the overall dreamy quality you got.', '2004-03-15 00:41:53.000000', NULL, 1),
(621146, 64516, 9232, '<td valign="top">Poor subject.  Naww, just kidding.  Good shot.  What were you using for the catchlights?  Octagonal softbox (the skin tones look pretty even) or a regular lamp of some kind?</td>', 'Poor subject.  Naww, just kidding.  Good shot.  What were you using for the catchlights?  Octagonal softbox (the skin tones look pretty even) or a regular lamp of some kind?', '2004-03-15 00:43:13.000000', NULL, 1),
